window.docContents[190].resolve({"/Elementary-Functions/___4___2-Definitions/#chapter-4-section-2":{"contents":"The DLMF distinguishes the multivalued logarithm Ln from its principal branch ln.\nThe general path avoids the origin and has a branch point at 0; the principal branch is analytic\non ℂ \\\\ (-∞,0] and real on positive reals. Figure 4.2.1\nshows the shared branch cut for ln z and z^α. We use the closed convention on the cut,\n-π < ph z ≤ π, with explicit upper and lower cut values. Mathlib Complex.log is totalized at\nzero, so the Lean statements retain the DLMF nonzero/domain hypotheses.\n\nThe general logarithm is the path integral Ln z = ∫₁ᶻ dt/t for z ≠ 0, with paths avoiding the origin.\nThe anchored Lean statement below is the algebraic principal branch-value specialization; the\npath-integral continuation and winding construction remain documented here in prose.\n\nThe principal logarithm is single-valued off the negative real axis.  Its phase lies in (-π,π)\nfor the open convention and in (-π,π] for the closed convention (the lower endpoint is excluded and π is included); the only zero of ln z is at z=1;\non the cut the upper and lower\nvalues differ by the sign of iπ, and the general value adds 2 k π i. Source links: E2, E3, E4, E5, E6, and E7.\n\nChanging the base gives logarithm quotients and the reciprocal/base-change identities.  The natural\nbase is the unique positive number e; the decimal formulas identify common and natural logarithms,\nincluding the displayed numerical constants. E11 retains a coarse enclosure of the displayed decimal\nprefix, while E15 includes both the quotient and (log₁₀ e) ln z equalities.\nSource links: E8, E9, E10, E11, E12, E13, E14, E15, E16, E17, and E18.\nThe reusable Lean API is logBase a z = Complex.log z / Complex.log a; its statements below\ncarry the source restrictions a,b ≠ 0,1 and the nonzero denominator conditions. Lean's field\ndivision is totalized, so these hypotheses are part of the mathematical interface rather than an\nimplicit convention.\n\nThe exponential is entire and has no real or complex zeros; it also has the displayed power series, period 2πi, reciprocal identity,\nmodulus exp(Re z), phase modulo 2π, Cartesian decomposition, and inverse relation with the general logarithm.\nSource links: E19, E20, E21, E22, E23, E24, and E25.\n\nPowers are defined through exp(a Ln z) in general and exp(a ln z) principally; E26 retains every\ninteger logarithm branch, while E28 onward specializes to the principal slit-plane branch. The section records\ninteger powers, modulus and phase formulas, the relation with e^z, factorial series for e, inversion,\nthe principal-value phase restriction -π ≤ Im((1/a)Ln w) ≤ π, and the closed-definition square-root example.\nThe principal power is analytic on ℂ \\ (-∞,0] and is two-valued and discontinuous on the cut unless\nthe exponent is integral. For the open convention, the final E37 inequalities become strict.\nE36 assumes the half-open principal-phase condition -π < Im((1/a) ln w) ≤ π and concludes\nthe corresponding principal-power inversion; it does not assert that this condition holds universally.\nSource links: E26, E27, E28, E29, E30, E31, E32, E33, E34, E35, E36, and E37.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions","header":"4.2. §4.2 Definitions","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___2-Definitions/#chapter-4-section-2"},"/Elementary-Functions/___4___7-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___7-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations--___4___7-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations--___4___7_LPAR_ii_RPAR_-Exponentials-and-Powers":{"contents":"\\frac{d}{dz}e^z=e^z.$$The complex exponential is its own derivative.\n\n\\frac{d}{dz}e^{az}=ae^{az}.$$The chain rule inserts the constant multiplier a.\n\n\\frac{d}{dz}a^z=a^z\\ln a,\\qquad a\\ne0.$$For principal complex powers, the logarithm is the principal Complex.log.\nDLMF's general-power note replaces it by the branch used to define the power.\n\n\\frac{d}{dz}z^a=az^{a-1}.$$On a domain contained in the principal slit plane, differentiating the fixed\nprincipal branch in the base gives the displayed rule.\n\n\\frac{d^n}{dz^n}z^a=a(a-1)\\cdots(a-n+1)z^{a-n}.$$The finite product records the descending factors for the n-th derivative on\nthe same explicit slit-plane branch domain.\n\n\\frac{dw}{dz}=f(z)w.$$This is the first-order linear equation whose coefficient is the function f.\nIt is represented as a candidate-solution predicate on a domain U.\n\nw=\\mathrm{exp}\\left(\\int f(z)\\,dz\\right)+\\text{constant}.$$Writing F' = f, the complete nonvanishing solution family on an open\nconnected domain is a constant multiplier C * exp(F), with C ≠ 0.\n\n\\frac{d^2w}{dz^2}=aw,\\qquad a\\ne0.$$The constant-coefficient second-order equation uses a nonzero complex constant a and\nis represented as a candidate predicate with a HasDerivAt second derivative.\n\nw=Ae^{\\sqrt a z}+Be^{-\\sqrt a z}.$$For nonzero a, with a chosen square-root branch satisfying (sqrt a)^2 = a, every solution on\nan open connected domain is the linear combination of the two exponential modes,\nwith constants A and B.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.7 Derivatives and Differential Equations\u0009§4.7 Derivatives and Differential Equations","header":"§4.7(ii) Exponentials and Powers","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___7-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___7-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations--___4___7-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations--___4___7_LPAR_ii_RPAR_-Exponentials-and-Powers"}});